      "role": "user",
      "content": "that makes a lot of sense. so basically i can keep my general expertise, just not their specific secret sauce. one more thing though—how do i actually prove what is a 'residual' (my general brain power/skill) versus what is 'confidential info' (their specific stuff) if they ever tried to come after me? is there a standard way to document these things to keep the line clear?",
      "source": "evaluator"
    },
    {
      "role": "assistant",
      "content": "Documenting the distinction between 'residuals' and confidential information is indeed important in managing your intellectual property independently. You might consider maintaining a detailed journal or log while working on the project. Record the general knowledge you apply from your previous work and other fields, and categorize the specific tips, techniques, and processes that you learned from the client as separate. This way, for every piece of code you write or every process you implement in your client's system, note the origin of your knowledge. If the item can be broadly replicated outside the client's environment and doesn't include their unique business logic or methods, it should be considered your general expertise. Conversely, elements directly derived from your client's proprietary methods should be marked as confidential.\n\nIn the event of a dispute, this detailed record-keeping will serve as a valuable reference to justify the ownership of your work outside the client's proprietary domain. It’s wise to integrate these practices into your standard documentation process to ensure clarity and tidiness. Consult your attorney to ensure what you're recording complies with contractual obligations and that it serves as an effective defensive measure should legal issues arise. Remember, seeking legal counsel is always recommended to help interpret NDA agreements and to provide guidance on protecting and documenting your intellectual property.",
